Subject: [PATCH 10730/11171] The duration-floor gap is categorical: work type,
 not model-was-loaded

exp6868 is a third distinct class. Its markers ARE in a declaration field, so
unlike the five data-marker instances this artifact genuinely runs the model. What
it runs is forced-sequence scoring -- logprobs over sequences that already exist,
no tokens generated -- at 51.36s against a 60s floor calibrated for generation.

Two substrates now straddle that line doing cheaper-than-generation work:
native_gguf_tokenization_without_inference at 38.4s, 51.7s and 140.8s, and
forced-sequence scoring at 51.4s flagged versus 75.1s clean. exp6850 and exp6868
are the same work class 24 seconds apart on opposite sides of the threshold.

So the taxonomy has one floor for "the model was loaded" and treats every use of a
loaded model as if it generated tokens. Generation, forced-sequence scoring,
embedding extraction and tokenization differ by more than an order of magnitude in
cost, and only embedding extraction was ever given its own floor, 2.0s on
2026-07-03. Choosing a new number per substrate one incident at a time will keep
reproducing this entry.

The entry says what this suggests and what it does not. It suggests keying the floor
on work type with a measured value each. It does not establish those numbers: four
observations across two substrates, and the cost of each work type at this model
size is unmeasured. The point of recording the shape is that the next incident adds
to a category instead of opening a sixth one-off.

Cost noted too: exp6868 needed three attempts -- a hard wall-clock kill at 4802s, an
artifact_not_updated_past_bootstrap FAIL, then the quarantine.

